WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
U.K. astronomers have traced the third known interstellar object back to its likely birthplace in another star system. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 189. U.K. astronomers combine trajectory modeling with spectral data from ground-based telescopes to narrow the likely-parent star system of interstellar comet 3i Atlas. The object is only the third confirmed interstellar visitor observed in our solar system. The analysis ruled out several nearby stars

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.840
as possible origins. Composition measurements showed the comet's volatile content differs from

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:46.240
typical solar system comets. The work adds constraints on how such objects are ejected from their birth systems. Students from Hong Kong and Macau joined a live

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:54.980
Tiangong Space Station class. The class featured experiments on fluid behavior in microgravity. The session highlighted how the station's crew

00:00:54.980 --> 00:01:02.840
conducts daily science operations. Organizers said the event aimed to spark interest in aerospace careers among younger audiences.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:11.880
The broadcast reached classrooms across both regions on the same day. This outreach connected students directly with ongoing space station activities.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:20.940
A large plume of dust crossed parts of Mali and neighboring countries. NASA's Earth Observatory captured the event from orbit during a period

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:27.840
when such activity normally declines. The image shows the plume stretching across national borders and into the Atlantic.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:36.140
Forecasters noted the timing was unusual for the season. Satellite views revealed the scale of the dust movement over West Africa.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:42.200
NASA Administrator Bill Nelson spoke with students about agency career paths and current missions.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:47.580
The visit included discussions on how university research supports NASA's science goals.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:56.180
Nelson highlighted opportunities in Earth science and human exploration programs. The event was part of a broader outreach effort at the campus.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:01.720
Students gained direct insight into how academic work feeds into ongoing space projects.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:08.900
The European Space Agency signed the agreement on behalf of Greece to enable participation in a private astronaut mission.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:13.640
The deal covers Greece's first astronaut flight to the International Space Station.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:21.440
VAST will provide the mission slot as part of its sixth private astronaut program. NASA is listed as a partner on the flight.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.140
The arrangement opens a pathway for Greek involvement in commercial crewed flights.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:32.200
NASA moved two Artemis II crew members into its Emeritus Astronaut program after their mission.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:37.080
The program recognizes astronauts who have completed their active flight assignments.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:41.520
Both individuals flew on the crewed lunar flyby earlier this year.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.080
The transition allows them to continue supporting NASA in advisory roles.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:50.040
Their experience will inform future planning for lunar operations.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:57.880
NASA awarded an on-ramp slot under its NLS-2 contract to Relativity Space for the Terran-R rocket.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.420
The vehicle will be available for future agency payloads once it reaches operational status.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.660
The contract structure allows multiple providers to compete for launches.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.160
No specific mission assignments were announced with the award.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:16.500
The addition expands the pool of launch options for NASA science and exploration missions.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.980
From one window into the galaxy's formation history,

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:25.440
to direct evidence of the universe's first minutes, observations of distant galaxies

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.320
confirmed the amount of helium produced in the first seconds after the Big Bang.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:34.380
The new data match long-standing predictions from Big Bang nucleosynthesis models.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:38.460
The measurements come from spectroscopy of pristine gas clouds.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.360
The results tighten constraints on the density of ordinary matter in the early cosmos.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:48.920
The agreement between observation and theory strengthens in the standard model of the universe's

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:53.800
first minutes. The universe produced most of its helium when it was roughly the size of our

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:59.800
solar system, and only a few minutes old. Every cubic meter of space back then held about as

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:06.100
much mass as a large city today. That density drove rapid fusion before expansion cooled everything

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:12.400
below the threshold for further reactions. In those first 180 seconds, protons and neutrons

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:18.160
combined into helium nuclei at a fixed ratio set by the temperature and the slight excess of

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:25.000
matter over antimatter. One in four nuclei by mass ended up as helium four, with trace amounts of

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:31.510
uterium and lithium formed alongside. The expansion rate determined exactly how much time was available

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:37.770
for these reactions to occur. We can still measure that exact ratio in the most untouched gas clouds

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:43.430
billions of light years away. The lingering puzzle is why the observed lithium abundance falls

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:49.190
short of the predicted value by a factor of three. Researchers propose that the little red dots

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:56.870
seen by JWST may be producing high energy neutrinos that reach Earth today. The galaxies formed in

00:04:56.870 --> 00:05:02.910
the early universe and appear unusually compact. Neutrino production would require intense particle

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:09.290
acceleration within these systems. The idea offers a new way to test models of early galaxy evolution.

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:15.950
Spectral features from JWST observations support the possibility of such energetic processes.

00:05:16.670 --> 00:05:21.990
After testing the universe's earliest chemistry, we turned to how planets themselves are consumed

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:27.730
by their stars. An international team developed a method that uses beryllium abundance to detect

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:33.570
stars that have consumed rocky planets. The technique was led by researchers at the University of

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:39.830
Sao Paulo. Higher beryllium levels indicate material from planetary interiors has mixed into the

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:45.830
stars atmosphere. The approach provides a new observational window on planetary system evolution.

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:51.170
Abundance measurements from high-resolution spectra form the basis of the detection method.

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:56.490
From stars devouring planets to possible refuges where life might survive on Mars,

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:02.690
tiny cracks inside salt crystals can trap liquid water and moderate temperatures at the same time

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:09.710
on Mars. A team at ELTE Oatvost-Loran University modeled how these microenvironments stay stable.

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:14.750
The process relies on deli quesence where salts absorb atmospheric moisture.

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:21.390
The study suggests such niches could allow microbial survival even when surface conditions are hostile.

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:26.350
Laboratory simulations of Martian salt chemistry guided the modeling effort.

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:31.650
From potential Martian habitats to unexpected x-ray sources in distant galaxies,

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:37.670
NASA's Chandra x-ray observatory detected a new class of objects that emit unusually low

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:44.630
energy x-rays alongside strong ultraviolet radiation. The sources appear in other galaxies and do

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:50.630
not match known categories. Astronomers suggest they may help explain both the origin of certain

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:56.870
x-ray populations and the nature of compact objects. Follow-up observations are planned to

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:02.090
determine their distances and luminosities. The unusual energy output distinguishes these

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:08.270
objects from previously catalogued x-ray sources. From mysterious compact objects to one reported

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:14.610
black hole merger that may not be so impossible after all. New calculations suggest the black holes

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:20.550
in a reported forbidden merger were smaller than earlier estimates. A spacetime warp could allow

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:26.570
the pair to form and merge despite previous mass constraints. The revised masses bring the event

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:32.690
back into line with known formation channels. The study uses updated models of binary evolution.

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:37.350
Revised mass estimates remove the need for exotic formation scenarios.

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:43.610
Low mass stars experience an abrupt brightness dip near the end of their lives known as the luminosity

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:49.330
bump. A study from the Heidelberg Institute for Theoretical Studies identifies entropy changes

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:54.610
as the driver of the bump's onset. The work appears in the astrophysical journal letters.

00:07:55.310 --> 00:08:01.350
The finding resolves why the dip begins at a particular evolutionary stage. Stellar interior models

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:06.650
incorporating entropy gradients reproduce the observed timing of the feature. Advanced

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:11.890
civilizations running hyper-efficient computers might radiate waste heat at specific wavelengths.

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:18.450
The idea builds on the observation that computation requires energy and produces detectable thermal

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:24.190
signatures. Researchers note that such signals would differ from natural astrophysical sources.

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:31.070
The concept offers one possible techno signature for future searches. Ways to heat modeling shows how

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:37.390
efficiency improvements could shift the expected emission spectrum. Next time, keep an eye on follow-up

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:42.330
observations that aim to pin down the distances of those new hypersoft x-ray sources.

